\title{Truffle is the wrong first tier for a proof checker, and a tracing compiler on the kernel loops was tried and removed}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{truffle}
\tag{platform}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/08-thc-truffle-tricks.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: [study-thc-truffle-tricks](study-thc-truffle-tricks) and [study-rpylean-and-tenet-jvm](study-rpylean-and-tenet-jvm); [the research session](ses-XOS5). Read from the repositories of two Truffle implementations of lazy functional languages and of rpylean; the numbers are theirs.}
\ul{
\li{Neither Truffle project shows that Truffle pays for a proof checker; both show what it costs. In one, compiled guest code is about 64 times faster than the same tree in the Truffle interpreter; in the other, one top-tier compilation of a small root had a median of 532 ms of compiler time in archived logs taken with graph dumping on. A root must run for a large fraction of a second in the interpreter before compiling it is a net win.}
\li{The Truffle interpreter is the wrong first tier: about 150 ns per guest call against about 2.4 ns compiled. Kernel checking is a workload where the amount of computation is small and the terms are large, so most code runs once.}
\li{Neutral terms routed through an exception cost nothing on closed code and make symbolic evaluation 16 to 28 times slower. Evaluation of open terms, the normal case of a kernel, needs first-class neutral values and belongs in the first tier.}
\li{The authors of rpylean put a tracing compiler on the reduction, comparison and substitution loops of their kernel and removed it: Init ran 136.7 s with the compiler off and 162.4 s with it on, about 15 percent of wall time on a megamorphic tree walk that the tracer cannot speed up. Their only traced loop today is a separate machine for closed terms, with an effect between -0.1 and +1.6 percent on library corpora.}
}
\p{The design consequence was a plain stepper as the first tier and Truffle as an optional accelerator for closed, hot code. The measurement on the built tier agrees: see [the Truffle tier](ins-KLLU).}
\p{See also: [the Truffle tier](design-truffle-tier); [code objects and tiers](design-code-objects-and-tiers).}
